    "lawCode": "Amd §865, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8228\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to eligibility for the\nshock program\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would expand eligibility for the shock incarceration program\nfor certain non-violent offenders.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would expand eligibility for the Shock Incarcera-\ntion Program to allow non-violent inmates who had been in prison previ-\nously for a non-violent offense to be reviewed by the Department of\nCorrectional Services (DOCS) for the Shock program.\n\nSection 2 of the bill is the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York State has the largest Shock Incarceration Program for sentenced\nstate prisoners in the nation. Shock is an intensive, \"boot camp\" based\nprogram available for male and female inmates convicted of non-violent\noffenses. Since screening of Shock eligible inmates began in July 1987,\nan average of 242 inmates are admitted to the program each month.\n\nThe Shock Incarceration Program saves the State money, while providing\ninmates intensive treatment and education programs. For the 35,102\nreleases from Shock through September 30, 2006, the Department saved an\nestimated $1.18 billion in both operating and capitol costs. Each year,\nsupervised crews of shock inmates perform thousands of hours of communi-\nty service as part of the daily routine of the facilities. It is esti-\nmated that in calendar year 2006 inmates from Shock facilities performed\n1.2 million hours of community service.\n\nEach Shock graduate was released to Parole supervision an average of 345\nor approximately 11.3 months earlier than his/her earliest possible\nrelease without Shock. On average, Shock inmates who graduated during\nfiscal year 2005-2006 increased their math and reading scores by at\nleast one grade level. The GED passing rate for Shock inmates was 80%,\nconsiderably higher than the passing rates at comparison medium (57%)\nand minimum (41%) facilities. Shock graduates are more likely than\ncomparison group parolees to be successful on parole supervision and\nmore successful at securing employment than comparison group parolees.\n\nPrior to last year's expansion of the Shock program, inmates could only\nbe placed into Shock immediately upon receipt by DOCS.  Therefore, the\nShock program essentially reduced a prison sentence to 6 months before\n\nrelease on parole. However, inmates can now \"age in\" to the program\nmeaning that an inmate can be placed in the program once they are within\n3 years of release after serving a portion of their prison sentence in\ngeneral confinement. Therefore, it no longer makes sense to limit eligi-\nbility to first-time offenders when second non-violent offenders could\nalso benefit from the program prior to release after serving a portion\nof their sentence.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nAccording to the Department of Correctional Services, there is existing\ncapacity on the Shock program to accommodate additional inmates so no\nfiscal impact is anticipated.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediate.\n\n",
